<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imbangala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th–17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Imbangala were bands of warriors in 16th- and 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, organised by initiation rather than kinship and bound by strict military laws called kijila. They allied at times with the Portuguese, raiding for captives for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, and also served Queen Njinga. Some bands founded kingdoms, notably Kasanje in the Kwango valley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imbangala.jpg" alt="Imbangala" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Theodor de Bry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RegCongo12.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origins of the Imbangala are still debated, though historians agree that they were not the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaga">Jagas</a> who attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a> under Alvaro I. In the 1960s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-vansina">Jan Vansina</a> and David Birmingham read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> oral traditions as tracing them to the Lunda Empire under a leader named Kinguri; another view makes them a local people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, from the Bie Plateau or the coast west of the highlands. The first eyewitness account came from the English sailor Andrew Battell, who lived among them for 16 months around 1600 to 1601 just south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwanza-river">Kwanza River</a> and whose story Samuel Purchas published in 1614 and 1625.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Battell describes bands that moved every few months, killing or enslaving those who could not flee. Children born inside the kilombo, the fortified camp, were killed so that kinship could not replace initiation, and the bands grew by capturing adolescents, trained daily in combat. A boy wore a collar until he had killed a man in battle. Fighters anointed themselves with maji a samba, believed to make them invulnerable as long as they kept codes demanding infanticide, cannibalism and absolute courage; Battell reports that cowards were killed and eaten.</p><p>The fighting men, ngunza, formed twelve squadrons, each under a captain called a musungo, and a kilombo had twelve gates, one for each. In the field they attacked with a right horn, a left horn and a vanguard in the centre, armed with bows, knives, swords and, above all, the war club or hatchet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Portuguese governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>, fought to a standstill by Ndongo, hired Imbangala bands from 1615, above all after Luis Mendes de Vasconcelos's assault on Ndongo in 1618, despite the protests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvaro-ii-of-kongo">Álvaro II of Kongo</a>. The bands proved hard to control. Kasanje's broke away and settled along the Kwango, while Kaza's joined Ndongo and then betrayed Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/njinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba">Njinga Mbande</a> in 1629; she later formed or joined a band of her own in Matamba. Other bands served as Portuguese auxiliaries or were destroyed, and south of the Kwanza they gradually joined states such as Bihe, Huambo and Bailundu, their customs moderating in the 18th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Imbangala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The balance shifted again when the Portuguese enlisted <b>Imbangala</b> war bands from 1615, and from 1617 Governor Luís Mendes de Vasconcelos invaded Ndongo and sacked Kabasa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ndongo">Kingdom of Ndongo · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade networks reached the Copperbelt and neighbouring polities, linking Lunda markets with the Luba, Luvale (Lwena) and other Central African peoples, and westwards through the <b>Imbangala</b> kingdom of Kasanje to Portuguese Angola.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Most were captured by other Africans in wars and raids, with <b>Imbangala</b> and Mbundu slave hunters, and sold to Luanda's merchants; Brazilian ships were the most numerous in Luanda and Benguela, so that Angola was in effect a colony of Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Kasanje was founded around 1620 by an <b>Imbangala</b> warband, formerly allied with Portuguese Angola, which settled in the Baixa de Kasanje, the fertile Kwango valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kasanje">Kingdom of Kasanje</a></li><li><blockquote>Njinga ruled with the support of <b>Imbangala</b> warriors, trading captives to the Dutch during their occupation of Luanda and later to the Portuguese after the peace of 1656.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-matamba">Kingdom of Matamba</a></li><li><blockquote>The colony grew from a coastal base through wars with Ndongo, alliances with <b>Imbangala</b> bands and the founding of Benguela in 1617, always driven by the demand for slaves in Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a></li></ul></section>
